WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:07.450
METE ATAMEL: Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.880
My name is Mete Atamel.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.400
I'm a Developer Advocate.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.860
And today, I want to talk about
an app modernization story

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.400
with Cloud Run.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.180
Back in early 2016, a
friend of mine and I--

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.050
we wanted to build a
news aggregator app.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.380
In this app, we would go
and fetch news articles

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.710
from various news
sources, and we

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.940
would display them in a mobile
app in a clean timeline.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.280
My friend worked on
the mobile front end

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.440
while I worked on the back end.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.070
In this presentation, I want
to talk about the modernisation

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.490
journey or the back end.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.000
The back end was initially
written with ASP.NET 4.6.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.140
It was a Windows app, and it
was deployed on an IIS host.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.330
There were three main services.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.170
The reader would fetch
the right size feeds.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.300
The transformer would convert
them into a common format

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.650
that we defined.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:06.490
And the web API would serve the
feeds to the mobile front end.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.700
The initial prototype, we worked
on, on a couple of weekends,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.180
and it worked.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.630
It was very easy to understand,
and it was very easier

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.160
to deploy because everything
was bundled together.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.320
Also, the IIS host
was quite inexpensive.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.070
At the same time, the app had
issues from the beginning.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:28.220
First of all, there was too
much coupling between services.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.370
All three services there
were all bundled together.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:35.150
The developer experience of
the IIS host was not great.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.390
Sometimes the machine where
our application was running on

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.150
would go down, and
we wouldn't know why.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.850
Or if we had to look at the
logs of the application,

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.960
I had to FTP into the machine
and download the logs.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.630
We didn't have any
kind of redundancy,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.230
any kind of persistence,
or any kind of resilience.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.890
So if there were any issues
with any of the services,

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.050
it could potentially bring
down our whole application.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.960
But this was the
initial prototype,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.620
and it was a good start.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.960
And it served us well
for about a year.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.810
But in 2017, we started looking
into moving our application

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.770
to a better place.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.500
That's when we came
across Compute Engine.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.400
Compute Engine-- they
are virtual machines

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.850
on Google Cloud
where you can have

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.200
as Linux or Windows-based
virtual machines.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.700
So we took our app and we
deployed to a Windows Server

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.510
on Compute Engine.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.150
Then we configured
it the way we liked.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.285
And then, we created what's
called an instance template out

00:02:36.285 --> 00:02:37.980
of our VM.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.370
Instance template is kind of
like a snapshot of your VM.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.810
And then you can use it to
create instance groups, which

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:50.400
are identical VMs load balanced
behind the Cloud Load Balancer.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.160
With this setup,
we got redundancy.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.620
We had two VMs running
our application,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.650
we got load balancing
with Cloud Load Balancer,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.190
and we also got
possibility of autoscaling

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.850
because with
instance groups, you

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.190
can set metrics and use
those metrics to autoscale

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.710
if you need to.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.060
It was a very easy move.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.617
We didn't have to
change our code.

00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:12.950
We just had to deploy it to
do Windows Server on Compute

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.160
Engine.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.470
And the developer
experience with Google Cloud

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.890
was much better.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:20.370
We had integrated
logging with Stackdriver,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.880
we had VM snapshots for
backup and recovery,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.120
and we had easy access to
our VMs with remote RDP.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.610
At the same time, things
were getting more expensive.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.540
Instead of running on
a single machine shared

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.310
by other applications, we were
running on two dedicated VMs,

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.780
and we had Cloud Load
Balancer as well.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.780
Even though it wasn't
that expensive,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.390
it was more than we wanted
for a hobby application.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.650
But this setup served us
well for a couple of years.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:56.660
From 2017 to 2019, there
were a few interesting things

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.480
happening in the tech world,
especially in .NET world.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.270
First of all, there was this new
version of the net called .NET

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.020
Core.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.320
And the most interesting thing
about .NET Core is that it can

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.210
run on multiple platforms--

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.520
Linux, Mac, or Windows.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.300
Secondly, there was a lot
of hype around containers.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.820
Everyone was talking about
how to take a single monolith

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.850
application running
on a VM and how

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.980
to break it down
into smaller pieces

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.380
and run them in containers
in a much more efficient way.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.730
Third, we had our
windows dependency.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.420
Our application was a
Windows application.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.940
And that limited our
deployment options,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.450
and we wanted to get rid
of that at some point.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.640
And last, it cost
our application.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.060
Even though it wasn't that
much, it was still something

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.170
that we wanted to bring down.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:53.810
So all of these forces came
into play, and in early 2019,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.070
we started looking into how to
containerize our application.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.220
Now, before containerization,
we had to first convert

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:04.260
our application from
.NET to .NET Core.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.030
So we rewrote our
application as .NET Core 2.2,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.310
the latest version at the time.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.650
And this took a weekend.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.660
We were pleasantly
surprised because we

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:19.680
were expecting at least couple
of weeks to do the conversion.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.950
But we had all the
libraries that we needed,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:26.030
and everything that
we were using on .NET,

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.610
they were already
available on .NET Core.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.030
Now, once we rewrote our
application on .NET Core,

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:35.900
we freed ourselves from Windows,
and we could run on Linux.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.800
That's when we got
into containerization.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.210
We containerized our
application with Docker

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.310
using some base images
provided by Microsoft.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.760
And then, we could
deploy to multiple places

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.290
in Google Cloud.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.980
There were two good options--

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.560
first, Google Kubernetes
Engine, and second, App Engine.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.780
We chose App Engine because all
we needed was a web front end.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.090
And everything was
bundled together.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.730
So App Engine served us well.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.410
Google Kubernetes
Engine could have

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.390
been better because it
provided infrastructure

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:10.340
for more complex architectures.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.140
But App Engine
was enough for us.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:15.840
So that's why we
went App Engine.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.020
With this setup, the Windows
license fees were out.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.940
So our application
was running on Linux,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.860
and it was cheaper to run.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.200
We got hassle-free autoscaling.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.870
Because in App Engine, you
get autoscaling without having

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.670
to do anything special.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.860
And we got the nice
features of App Engine,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:36.750
such as revision management
and traffic splitting.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.740
But we were still running
on virtual machines.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.570
App Engine does a good job of
hiding the fact that you are

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.460
running on virtual machines.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.310
But behind the scenes,
on App Engine Flex,

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:48.810
you are running on
virtual machines,

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:53.040
and you need to pay those
virtual machines per second.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.120
This meant that our
application, when it was not

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.240
used-- especially
during the evenings-- we

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.040
were still paying for
those VMs at all times.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.190
Also, the deployments
of App Engine--

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.310
there were kind of slow.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:10.820
It could take anywhere
from 5 to 10 minutes.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.560
Even though it
wasn't a big problem,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.980
it slowed us down during
our development cycle.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:22.250
Around mid-2019, something new
came along called Cloud Run.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.210
And the whole
premise of Cloud Run

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.020
is to bring serverless
features to containers.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.720
This was very exciting for
us, because our application

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.730
was already containerized.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.250
And we were already
looking into running it

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.300
in a serverless way, especially
when it comes to billing.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.460
So in mid-2000-- also, I
should mention that Cloud Run,

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.290
it has a couple of use cases.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.430
First one is public endpoints.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.430
Cloud Run is very good for that.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.000
And also, the second use
case is private endpoints

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.620
where you need to do some
kind of background task.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.910
Cloud Run is a good
fit for that as well.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.780
And both of these use cases were
the use cases or application.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.130
We had the web front
end that was public.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:07.800
And we had internal
services that

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.920
could be private
endpoints on Cloud Run.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:15.330
And also, the best part of
Cloud Run was its billing time.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.600
In Cloud Run, even
though your container

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.300
might be running for a while,
you only pay for the time

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.670
that the requests
are being handled.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.800
This meant that our
application, when

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.470
it wasn't being used
in the evenings,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.150
it was scaled down to
zero and we paid nothing.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:36.400
So with this in mind, we tried
to deploy our application

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.520
to Cloud Run in mid-2019.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.490
To do that, first, we
upgraded our application

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:46.160
to the latest version
of ASP.NET Core 3.0.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.260
And then, deploying to Cloud
Run was a single command--

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.630
gcloud app run deployed,
and it was there.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.060
You didn't have to
change anything.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.580
Now with this, we got
serverless billing.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:01.932
And this really brought down
our billing considerably.

00:09:01.932 --> 00:09:04.390
Because we weren't paying for
anything when our application

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.560
wasn't being used.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.940
We also got awesome developer
experience with Cloud Run.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:14.290
We had fast deploys, so we
could deploy within seconds

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:15.940
instead of minutes.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.640
And we got integrated
logging with Stackdriver.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.190
And we got all the awesome
features of Cloud Run

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:26.020
with traffic management, and
revision management, and so on.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.830
At the same time, we
were still running

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.290
as a single monolithic
application.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.750
And we had monolith issues.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.830
Those monolith issues
were, first, scaling.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:41.830
We could only scale
all or nothing.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.310
If we had to scale the web
front end because there

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.970
were more users, we had to scale
the reader and the converter

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:49.440
as well.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:51.210
And this wasn't ideal
for us because we

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.150
had to scale individual
services as we needed.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.420
But we couldn't do that
in a monolith application.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.930
Second, we had an
issue with cold starts.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.090
So when our application
wasn't being used,

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:03.620
it was scaled down to zero.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.600
And this was great for billing.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.360
But at the same time, when
the first user came along,

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:10.400
our application would wake up.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.330
It didn't have any
kind of persistence.

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.540
So it had to go and
fetch the feeds,

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.430
then it had to
transform them, and it

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.690
had to serve to the user.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:22.200
So this meant that
the first user could

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.820
wait for a while for
the data to show up,

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:28.190
and this was
becoming a big issue.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.770
Third, all of the
state was in memory.

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.530
We didn't have any
kind of backup state.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.580
And we wanted to have
something more persistent.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:41.030
And lastly, we didn't have a way
to update individual services.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:42.700
Most of the times,
we didn't have

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.520
to update our reader
or our web API,

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.090
but we had to change
rules about how

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.830
to transform their RSS feeds.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.450
Even though we
only had to change

00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:54.810
the small piece or
our location, we

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:56.410
had to update our
whole application

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.510
because it was a monolith.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.810
So with these issues
in mind, in stage four,

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.810
we started looking into
how to break down monolith

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:06.950
into smaller microservices.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:09.340
This was early 2020.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:11.710
And this is what
we came up with.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:14.710
First, we had this new
service called Reader.

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.650
This is a private
Cloud Run service.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:20.550
It's private, meaning it
doesn't have a public endpoint.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.600
And all it does is,
it goes and fetches

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:25.420
RSS feeds and brings them back.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.450
Now, this Reader is being
called by Cloud Scheduler, which

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:31.560
is a scheduling service
on Google Cloud.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.610
And Scheduler would call Reader
once an hour, every hour,

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.650
and Reader would go
and fetch the feeds.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:41.110
And it would save the
feeds into Cloud Storage,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:44.770
which is a cloud-based
object storage service.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:49.120
Now, once the fees are
saved into Cloud Storage,

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.280
Cloud Storage can
generate notifications

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:53.360
to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.240
And these
notifications, they can

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:59.130
be served to another Cloud
Run service called Converter.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:03.940
This Converter, it's another
private Cloud Run service.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:06.730
And it will look at the list
of rules that we defined,

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:10.600
and it will transform these RSS
feeds from different formats

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.060
into a common format
that we defined.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.490
Once they're put into
the common format,

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.920
they're saved into
Cloud Firestore.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.950
This is a fully
managed, NoSQL database.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:24.940
And it's perfect
for JSON-like data.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:29.170
And then, we had another public
Cloud Run service called Web.

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:32.210
This was the public front
end of our application.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.930
The users-- the end
users, the mobile app,

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.740
would go and ask for the
feeds from the web service.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.920
And the web would
just look at Firestore

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:44.270
and fetch the latest
data from there.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.300
As you can see, we
brought our monolith

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.840
into three separate
microservices.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:51.130
Two of them are private
services because they

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:52.600
don't need to be public.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.150
One of them is public.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.780
They are totally
independent from each other.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.200
So the reader doesn't have
to know about the Converter,

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.570
and the Web doesn't need
to care about the Converter

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:03.950
or the Reader.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.610
They are all
event-driven, so they

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:09.820
don't need to call each
other in a synchronous way.

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:12.130
And they can be
individually scaled.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:17.140
So if we have more users, we
can only up-scale web front end.

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:20.080
We don't have to scale the
Reader or the Converter.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:23.220
And they can be individually
updated as well.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.720
Usually, they'll need to
update Reader and Web.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.700
Because the way we read
and the way we serve

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.100
is always the same.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.510
But we have to update
Converter quite often.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:36.430
Because the rules around RSS
feeds, they change quite a lot.

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.590
And this setup
allows us to do that.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:40.840
So with this setup, even
though it's a little bit more

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.810
complicated, we
so all the issues

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:46.470
that we had with the
previous application.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:49.600
Now, the lessons learned in
this transformation journey--

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:52.180
first of all,
transformation does not

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:53.860
have to be all or nothing.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.340
As you can see from
our journey, it

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.720
was a step-by-step
transformation

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.680
where each step added something
new to our application,

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:08.060
removed some legacy, and opened
the way for new technologies.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:10.820
Secondly, sometimes
you need to use

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.950
non-optimal optimal
solutions to get ready

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:14.600
for optimal solutions.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:17.600
For example, in our case,
when you look at it,

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:21.530
App Engine Flex was probably
not the most optimal solution

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.990
because we were
still running on VMs.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:25.910
Cloud Run was the
optimal solution

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:29.690
because we needed an
event-driven microservices

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.750
kind of environment.

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.710
And that's what Cloud
Run allowed us to do.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:37.670
But to get from our
VM to Cloud Run,

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:39.990
we had to use App
Engine in the middle.

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:44.480
And by doing that, we went from
a .NET Windows app to a .NET

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.760
Core app running on App Engine.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:49.190
It wasn't ideal, but
it served us well

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:51.380
because in the
next iteration, we

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:55.430
could much easier move from
App Engine Flex to Cloud Run.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:59.720
Third, even simple things like
lift and shift to the cloud

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:01.445
can have huge benefits.

00:15:01.445 --> 00:15:06.290
In our application, by moving
from IAS to Google Cloud,

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.210
we didn't have to really
change any of our code.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.140
All we had to change was
our deployment and then

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:13.460
where we deployed.

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:16.700
But by doing that, we had
instance tablets, instance

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:18.680
groups, and load balancing.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.940
And with those, we got more
resilience and redundancy.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:26.000
So, to get benefits,
you don't always

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.050
need to change your code.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:28.550
You can just change
your environment

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:30.990
and still get those benefits.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:35.070
And at some point, you should
expect some kind of rewrite.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.890
Especially if you want
to use the benefits

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.090
of the new environment
that you're running in.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.830
For example, in our case,
when we deployed to Cloud Run,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:46.440
initially, we deployed
as a single monolith.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.720
We got some benefits,
but it wasn't

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.580
all the benefits of Cloud Run.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.520
To truly use the
benefits of Cloud Run,

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:56.940
we had to actually decompose our
application into microservices.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:59.190
And we had to make
them event driven.

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.800
And for that, we had
to restructure our code

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.450
and had to break it down.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.960
So it was a lot of
work that was needed

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:12.370
in order to use Cloud Run in
a way that made sense for us.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:14.680
And we all talk about
monolith applications,

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.860
and how they're bad, and how
we should break them down

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:18.920
into smaller pieces.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:20.810
And it's usually true.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.710
But at the same time,
this is a hard process.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:27.820
It involves refactoring and
restructuring your code.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.370
And then it involves
figuring out

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:34.670
how these new services will
communicate with each other,

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:36.370
whether it's going
to be event driven,

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:38.740
or whether it's going
to be synchronous,

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:40.790
and what kind of storage
they're going to use.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:43.680
So there's all these questions
that you need to answer.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.960
Monolith decomposition--
it's something

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.670
that you should take
it very seriously.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.560
And you should have
a really good reason

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:53.560
to do it if you want to do it.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:55.720
So that's all I have for today.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.480
Hopefully, this was useful.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.050
And if you want to read
more about this application,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:03.510
I wrote a blog
post series where I

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.329
explain in more detail what
this application is about

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:08.160
and give you more
details about it.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:09.694
And the code is,
as well, on GitHub.

00:17:09.694 --> 00:17:11.819
So if you want to take a
look at the back end code,

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:13.060
it's out there.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.859
And thanks very
much for listening.

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:16.490
Thank you.

